        {
          "type": "agentMessage",
          "id": "msg_0d498ec2449fdb88016ab8f8aa4ea487d185172b2a7312ccab",
          "text": "Yes. **You explored four main approaches**, plus k-means as a reference. Here is what the conversations report; I haven’t rerun them.\n\n| Approach | Idea | What happened |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Deforming hexagons** | Move shared corners to balance the populations inside cells. | Early rules stalled. Later coordinated moves and local annealing reportedly reached **157–158 dots in each of 38 cells**. Boundary checks were still global. |\n| **Overlapping disks** | Move and resize disks; split each dot’s weight among containing disks. | Reached **±10% balance** on the original dots. Later driver experiments exposed disconnected groups; removing the neighbor limit restored balance but produced huge overlaps. |\n| **Non-overlapping disks** | Allow gaps; assign uncovered dots to the nearest center. | Discussed, then replaced by the mesh approach. No completed result found. |\n| **Centers on a honeycomb** | Each center pools dots with three neighbors, moves toward their pooled centroid, then redistributes. | Simple and local, but remained substantially unbalanced: **17–18 empty centers out of 100**. |\n\nThe **3D triangle idea** was an earlier positioning rule within the last approach. It was replaced by the pooled-centroid rule.\n\nMy reading:\n\n- **Hexagons gave the strongest reported balance**, but a fully local implementation remains unfinished.\n- **Disks made balance easier by sharing load**, sometimes at the expense of useful geography.\n- **The latest centroid rule simplified movement but stopped directly optimizing balance.** Its weaker balance is therefore unsurprising.\n- **K-means favored short distances.** The corrected 100-center comparison beat the latest local rule on both distance and balance.\n\nYour central unresolved problem is: **find a simple local rule that balances loads without destroying geographic usefulness.** The hexagon result is the strongest starting point in the existing record.",
          "phase": "final_answer"
        }
      ]
    },
